    "engine": "knitr",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Week 19 Self-Assessment\"\nsubtitle: \"APA Reporting Format\"\n---\n\n\n\n## Instructions\n\nTest your understanding of APA format for reporting statistical results. Correct reporting is essential for scientific communication and is assessed in your lab report and exam.\n\n---\n\n## Part 1: Conceptual Questions\n\n### Question 1: Why APA Format?\n\nAPA format for statistics is important because:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>It makes papers look professional</option><option value='answer'>It provides a standardised way to communicate findings that all researchers understand</option><option value=''>Journals require it for publication</option><option value=''>All of the above</option></select>\n\n### Question 2: General Formatting Rules\n\nIn APA format, statistical symbols (like *t*, *p*, *r*, *M*, *SD*) should be:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Bold</option><option value='answer'>Italicised</option><option value=''>Underlined</option><option value=''>In quotation marks</option></select>\n\nHow many decimal places are typically reported for means and standard deviations?\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>0</option><option value=''>1</option><option value='answer'>2</option><option value=''>As many as available</option></select>\n\n### Question 3: Leading Zeros\n\nFor values that cannot exceed 1 (like *p*, *r*, and proportions), APA format:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Always uses a leading zero (0.05)</option><option value='answer'>Does not use a leading zero (.05)</option><option value=''>Uses both interchangeably</option><option value=''>Uses percent instead</option></select>\n\nWhich is correct for reporting a p-value?\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>p = 0.032</option><option value='answer'>p = .032</option><option value=''>P = .032</option><option value=''>p-value = 0.032</option></select>\n\n### Question 4: Very Small p-Values\n\nWhen a p-value is very small (e.g., .00004), the correct way to report it is:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>p = .00004</option><option value=''>p = .000</option><option value='answer'>p < .001</option><option value=''>p is significant</option></select>\n\n### Question 5: Degrees of Freedom\n\nDegrees of freedom should be reported:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>At the end of the sentence</option><option value='answer'>In parentheses after the test statistic symbol</option><option value=''>Only if requested</option><option value=''>In a separate table</option></select>\n\n### Question 6: Rounding Rules\n\nValues should generally be rounded to:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Whole numbers</option><option value='answer'>Two decimal places</option><option value=''>Four decimal places</option><option value=''>The nearest tenth</option></select>\n\nWhen should you use three decimal places?\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>For all values</option><option value='answer'>For very small p-values (e.g., p = .002) or when more precision is needed</option><option value=''>Never</option><option value=''>Only for correlations</option></select>\n\n---\n\n## Part 2: Interpretation - Reporting t-Tests\n\n### Question 7: Independent t-Test Format\n\nThe correct APA format for an independent t-test is:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>t = 2.45, p = .018</option><option value='answer'>t(48) = 2.45, p = .018, d = 0.71</option><option value=''>t-test: 2.45, significant</option><option value=''>t(n=50) = 2.45, sig = .018</option></select>\n\n### Question 8: Paired t-Test Format\n\nA paired t-test with 25 participants would be reported as:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>t(25) = 3.12, p = .004</option><option value='answer'>t(24) = 3.12, p = .004, d = 0.62</option><option value=''>t = 3.12, df = 25, p = .004</option><option value=''>paired t = 3.12</option></select>\n\nWhy df = 24 for n = 25 in a paired test?\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>It's a mistake</option><option value='answer'>For paired tests, df = n - 1</option><option value=''>Half the sample is in each condition</option><option value=''>One participant was excluded</option></select>\n\n### Question 9: Negative t-Values\n\nWhen reporting t(28) = -2.89, should the negative sign be included?\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>No, always report absolute values</option><option value='answer'>Yes, it indicates the direction of the effect</option><option value=''>Only if specifically asked</option><option value=''>Negative values are errors</option></select>\n\n### Question 10: Complete t-Test Reporting\n\nWhich is the most complete and correct report?\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nopts_t <- c(\n  \"The t-test was significant (p < .05)\",\n  \"t = 2.45, p = .018\",\n  answer = \"An independent samples t-test revealed a significant difference, t(48) = 2.45, p = .018, d = 0.71\",\n  \"There was a significant effect of condition on the DV\"\n)\n```\n:::\n\n\n<div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_CLXDBIYRHT'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_CLXDBIYRHT\" value=\"\"></input> <span>The t-test was significant (p < .05)</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_CLXDBIYRHT\" value=\"\"></input> <span>t = 2.45, p = .018</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_CLXDBIYRHT\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>An independent samples t-test revealed a significant difference, t(48) = 2.45, p = .018, d = 0.71</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_CLXDBIYRHT\" value=\"\"></input> <span>There was a significant effect of condition on the DV</span></label></div>\n\n\n---\n\n## Part 3: Interpretation - Reporting Correlations\n\n### Question 11: Correlation Format\n\nThe correct APA format for a correlation is:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>r = .45, significant</option><option value='answer'>r(48) = .45, p = .001</option><option value=''>correlation = 0.45, p-value = 0.001</option><option value=''>Pearson r: .45*</option></select>\n\n### Question 12: Degrees of Freedom for Correlations\n\nFor a correlation with n = 50, df =\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>50</option><option value=''>49</option><option value='answer'>48</option><option value=''>100</option></select>\n\nThe formula is: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>df = n</option><option value=''>df = n - 1</option><option value='answer'>df = n - 2</option><option value=''>df = n/2</option></select>\n\n### Question 13: Reporting Correlation Strength\n\nWhen describing r = -.62 in text, you might write:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>A weak negative correlation</option><option value='answer'>A moderate-to-strong negative correlation</option><option value=''>A strong positive correlation</option><option value=''>No correlation</option></select>\n\n### Question 14: R-Squared Reporting\n\nIf you report r2, how many decimal places?\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>0</option><option value=''>1</option><option value='answer'>2 (e.g., r2 = .36)</option><option value=''>4</option></select>\n\nReporting both r and r2:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Is redundant and unnecessary</option><option value='answer'>Can be useful as r2 shows proportion of variance explained</option><option value=''>Is required in all cases</option><option value=''>Is incorrect</option></select>\n\n---\n\n## Part 3: Interpretation - Reporting Descriptive Statistics\n\n### Question 15: Means and SDs\n\nThe correct format for reporting a mean and standard deviation is:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>mean = 45.30, std = 8.25</option><option value=''>Average: 45.30 (8.25)</option><option value='answer'>M = 45.30, SD = 8.25</option><option value=''>45.30 +/- 8.25</option></select>\n\n### Question 16: Reporting Groups\n\nWhen comparing two groups, descriptive statistics should:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Only be in a table</option><option value='answer'>Be reported for each group, either in text or in a table</option><option value=''>Only include the combined mean</option><option value=''>Be omitted if there's a significant difference</option></select>\n\nExample: \"The experimental group (*M* = 72.40, *SD* = 8.50) scored higher than the control group (*M* = 65.20, *SD* = 9.10).\"\n\nIs this correct? <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>TRUE</option><option value=''>FALSE</option></select>\n\n---\n\n## Part 4: Application Questions\n\n### Question 17: Correcting Errors\n\nIdentify what is WRONG with each report:\n\n\"t(48) = 2.45, p = 0.018\" - Error: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Wrong degrees of freedom</option><option value='answer'>Leading zero on p-value</option><option value=''>Missing effect size</option><option value=''>All of these</option></select>\n\n\"p = .000\" - Error: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Wrong symbol</option><option value='answer'>Should be p < .001 (p cannot equal exactly zero)</option><option value=''>Too many zeros</option><option value=''>Needs italics</option></select>\n\n\"The correlation was r = .45 (p = significant)\" - Error: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Correlation is wrong</option><option value='answer'>Should report exact p-value, not just 'significant'</option><option value=''>r should be italicised</option><option value=''>Multiple errors</option></select>\n\n### Question 18: Writing a Complete Results Sentence\n\nFill in the blanks for correct APA format:\n\n\"An independent samples t-test revealed that participants in the music condition (<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>M = 72.40, SD = 8.50</option><option value=''>mean = 72.40, std = 8.50</option><option value=''>72.40 (8.50)</option><option value=''>M: 72.40</option></select>) recalled significantly more words than those in the silence condition (<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>M = 65.20, SD = 9.10</option><option value=''>mean = 65.20</option><option value=''>65.20 average</option><option value=''>M = 65.20 standard deviation = 9.10</option></select>), <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>t = 2.89, p = .006</option><option value='answer'>t(48) = 2.89, p = .006, d = 0.82</option><option value=''>t-test significant</option><option value=''>t(48) = 2.89, significance = .006</option></select>.\"\n\n### Question 19: Results vs Discussion Content\n\nWhich belongs in the Results section?\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>t(48) = 2.89, p = .006, d = 0.82</option><option value=''>This finding supports Smith's (2020) theory</option><option value=''>The study had several limitations</option><option value=''>Future research should examine...</option></select>\n\nWhich belongs in the Discussion section?\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>M = 45.30, SD = 8.25</option><option value='answer'>These results are consistent with previous research showing...</option><option value=''>r(48) = .45, p = .001</option><option value=''>The experimental group scored higher</option></select>\n\n---\n\n## Part 5: MCQ Exam Practice\n\n::: {.webex-check .webex-box}\n\n### Scenario 1: Report the t-Test\n\nIndependent t-test results: Group A (n=25): M=78.4, SD=10.2; Group B (n=25): M=72.1, SD=11.5; t=2.05, p=.046, d=0.58\n\n1. **Degrees of freedom:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>50</option><option value='answer'>48</option><option value=''>25</option><option value=''>24</option></select>\n\n2. **Correct p-value format:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>p = 0.046</option><option value='answer'>p = .046</option><option value=''>p < .05</option><option value=''>sig = .046</option></select>\n\n3. **Effect size is:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Small</option><option value='answer'>Medium</option><option value=''>Large</option><option value=''>Not reported</option></select>\n\n4. **Complete correct report:**\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nopts_report <- c(\n  \"t = 2.05, p = .046\",\n  \"t(50) = 2.05, p = 0.046, d = 0.58\",\n  answer = \"t(48) = 2.05, p = .046, d = 0.58\",\n  \"The groups were significantly different\"\n)\n```\n:::\n\n\n<div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_NMGCUNZAKX'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_NMGCUNZAKX\" value=\"\"></input> <span>t = 2.05, p = .046</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_NMGCUNZAKX\" value=\"\"></input> <span>t(50) = 2.05, p = 0.046, d = 0.58</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_NMGCUNZAKX\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>t(48) = 2.05, p = .046, d = 0.58</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_NMGCUNZAKX\" value=\"\"></input> <span>The groups were significantly different</span></label></div>\n\n\n:::\n\n::: {.webex-check .webex-box}\n\n### Scenario 2: Report the Correlation\n\nCorrelation results: n=60, r=.42, p=.0008\n\n1. **Degrees of freedom:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>60</option><option value=''>59</option><option value='answer'>58</option><option value=''>42</option></select>\n\n2. **How should p be reported?** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>p = .0008</option><option value=''>p = 0.001</option><option value='answer'>p < .001</option><option value=''>p = .001</option></select>\n\n3. **Correct report:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>r = .42, p < .001</option><option value='answer'>r(58) = .42, p < .001</option><option value=''>correlation = .42, significant</option><option value=''>r(60) = .42, p = .0008</option></select>\n\n4. **Describing the relationship:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Weak</option><option value='answer'>Moderate positive</option><option value=''>Strong</option><option value=''>Negative</option></select>\n\n:::\n\n::: {.webex-check .webex-box}\n\n### Scenario 3: Spot the Errors\n\nA student writes: \"We found a significant effect (t = 2.34, p = 0.023). The mean for Group 1 was 56.7 and Group 2 was 48.2.\"\n\nIdentify the errors:\n\n1. **Missing from t-test report:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Degrees of freedom and effect size</option><option value=''>The p-value</option><option value=''>Significance statement</option><option value=''>Group labels</option></select>\n\n2. **p-value format error:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Should not have leading zero (should be p = .023)</option><option value=''>Should be p < .05</option><option value=''>Should be rounded</option><option value=''>No error</option></select>\n\n3. **Descriptive statistics error:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Missing SDs and proper formatting (M = , SD = )</option><option value=''>Means are wrong</option><option value=''>Should only report one group</option><option value=''>Numbers should be whole</option></select>\n\n:::\n\n---\n\n## Summary Checklist\n\nBefore writing your Results section, verify:\n\n- [ ] Statistical symbols are italicised (*t*, *p*, *r*, *M*, *SD*, *d*)\n- [ ] Degrees of freedom in parentheses after test statistic\n- [ ] No leading zeros for values that cannot exceed 1 (*p*, *r*)\n- [ ] Two decimal places for most values\n- [ ] *p* < .001 for very small p-values (never *p* = .000)\n- [ ] Effect sizes reported\n- [ ] Descriptive statistics (M, SD) for each group/condition\n- [ ] Results reported without extensive interpretation\n\n::: {.callout-important}\n## Common Mistakes to Avoid\n1. Writing \"p = 0.023\" instead of \"p = .023\"\n2. Writing \"p = .000\" instead of \"p < .001\"\n3. Forgetting degrees of freedom: \"t = 2.45\" instead of \"t(48) = 2.45\"\n4. Omitting effect sizes\n5. Not italicising statistical symbols\n:::\n",
